# Deviations from analysis_plan.md (plan-v1)

No mortality outcome data had been accessed at the time of the entries below.

## 2026-11-17: Age variable for population definition and age adjustment

Plan: "Adults aged 20 years and older at the MEC examination".

Finding: RIDAGEEX (age at examination, in months) is missing for all 1,443 examined participants aged 80 years or older. In the pooled 2007-2014 demographics files it is also missing for 18,501 of 37,723 examined participants younger than 80.

Decision: use RIDAGEYR (age in years at the screening interview, top-coded at 80) for the age 20+ restriction and for the age spline.

Reason: RIDAGEYR is complete, whereas RIDAGEEX would drop the oldest participants and about half of the examined sample.

Consequences: age is measured at the screening interview rather than at examination (usually a difference of a few weeks). Top-coding at 80 limits resolution of age within the 80+ group. This will be noted as a limitation.

Scope: no change to the research question, exposures, outcome, adjustment sets or models.

## 2026-11-17: Operational definitions fixed before outcome linkage (no change to plan)

MORTSTAT and PERMTH_EXM were not examined at the time of these entries; only ELIGSTAT was linked.

- Race/ethnicity: RIDRETH1 (RIDRETH3 is not available in 2007-2010).
- Education: DMDEDUC2 in four categories; refused and don't know coded as missing.
- Income-poverty ratio: INDFMPIR categorised as <1.3, 1.3-3.49, >=3.5 (NHANES top-codes at 5).
- Physical activity: five domains from PAQ605-PAD675; if any domain is unknown (refused, don't know, or time set to missing by NCHS), total activity is missing. Gate answer "no" counts as 0 minutes. S2 cut-points as planned.
- Alcohol: ALQ101 = 9 or ALQ110 = 9 treated as missing, following the plan's lifetime-drinking rule (this includes 10 participants with ALQ101 = 9 and ALQ110 = 1). Codes 777 and 999 in ALQ120Q and ALQ130 treated as missing.
- Pregnancy: RIDEXPRG = 1 excluded; RIDEXPRG = 3 (cannot ascertain) retained.
- Participant flow: 22,673 examined adults aged 20+; 22,632 with ELIGSTAT = 1; 22,385 not pregnant; 18,155 complete cases (18.9% excluded). The 10% rule in the plan is therefore met, and multiple imputation will be run as a sensitivity analysis.
- Added limitations: public-use mortality files are perturbed by NCHS to reduce re-identification risk; linkage through 31 December 2019 is used as pre-specified, although NCHS has since updated linkage through 2022.

## 2026-11-17: Modelling details fixed before running any outcome model (no change to plan)

- Age spline: natural cubic spline with 4 df. Interior knots at the 25th, 50th and 75th percentiles of age in the primary complete-case population; boundary knots at its minimum and maximum (expected 20 and 80). The same basis is used in all models, including sensitivity analyses.
- Confidence intervals: Wald intervals using the t distribution with design degrees of freedom.
- Hazard ratios are reported only for the exposure of interest in each model.

## 2026-11-17: Implementation of the proportional hazards test (no change to plan)

The plan specifies exposure-by-log-follow-up-time interaction terms with design-based Wald tests (survey::regTermTest). To avoid relying on time-transform terms inside svycoxph, follow-up is split into 6-month episodes and each exposure is interacted with the log of the episode midpoint. Tests use the primary M3 adjustment sets and a joint Wald test of all interaction terms per exposure. Proportional hazards is considered not supported for an exposure if the joint p-value is below 0.05. In that case the pre-specified five-year split is applied and interval-specific hazard ratios are reported. Episode splitting leaves the partial likelihood unchanged; this is checked by reproducing the M3 smoking estimate on the split data.